{
 "id": "M01",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Comments add 8.6 points of recall but only 11 new catches",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 125,
 "caption": "Each of 500 backdoored programs falls in exactly one bar: the set of detectors that flag it. Three detectors, a keyword regex on the generator's own comments and the same monitor reading the code with comments stripped or kept. Bars do not overlap and sum to 500. Use the dial to move both monitor cutoffs.",
 "notes": [
  "At a 2% budget the comment-reading monitor catches 468 of 500 against 425 for the stripped one. Most of that gain is on backdoors a six-word regex would have flagged anyway; only 11 are caught by the comment-reading monitor alone.",
  "The regex bar does not move with the dial. A keyword match has no budget to spend, so only the two monitor cutoffs shift, which is why the bars redistribute rather than all grow.",
  "27 programs are caught by nothing at any setting shown. Those are the ones the hard-backdoor figures are about.",
  "Both monitors are Haiku 5.5 at xhigh effort on the same 500 problems, each calibrated on its own honest scores, with a strict greater-than cutoff. The only difference between them is whether the code arrived with its comments.",
  "Rebuilt from committed CSVs, replacing the exported snapshot taken from the data explorer."
 ],
